s"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/calculi.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/refactor.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/refactor-matchings.css"><script defer src="/assets/splice-a-signed-cycle.js"></script><script defer src="/assets/analytics.js" data-tensorgami-analytics="v1"></script></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><div class="wrap"><header class="masthead"><a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Refactor home"><img class="brand-emblem" src="/assets/brand/refactor-mark.svg?v=8be7135d0d95" width="48" height="48" alt="Refactor monogram" fetchpriority="high">Refactor</a><nav class="nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/papers">Research</a><a href="/expositions" aria-current="true">Mathematics</a><a href="/essays">Essays</a><a href="/about">About</a></nav></header><main id="main"><header class="refactor-article-heading"><p class="eyebrow"><a href="/expositions">Mathematics</a> / Groups · Signed permutations</p><h1>Splice a signed cycle</h1><p class="lead">Turn a signed commutator equation into two marked matchings. Splice the relabeling into a single negative cycle and get a shortest first factor.</p><p class="byline">Henry Shin · November 21, 2026 · 11 min read</p><p class="reader-background"><strong>Begin with:</strong> Permutation cycles and composition. Signed permutations and reflection length are introduced in the article.</p><div class="text-links"><a href="/assets/refactor/splice-a-signed-cycle.md" download>Download reading text</a><a href="/expositions">All mathematics</a></div></header><div class="refactor-reading-layout"><aside class="refactor-reading-sidebar"><nav aria-label="Article contents"><a href="#experiment">Join three cycles into one</a><a href="#splicing">Change the cycles without changing the conjugate</a><a href="#paths">Make the paths from the target</a><a href="#statement">The signed theorem and its sharp bound</a><a href="#sources-title">Sources</a></nav><a href="/refactor">The Refactor approach</a></aside><article class="refactor-prose"><p>Suppose you want to write a permutation as a commutator <var>XYX</var><sup>−1</sup><var>Y</var><sup>−1</sup>. You get to choose the two factors, but there are two restrictions. <var>X</var> must square to the identity and use as few swaps as possible. <var>Y</var> must visit every letter in a single cycle. Jacques, Lenormand, Lentin and Perrot proved in 1968 that every even permutation admits such a factorization. <a href="https://hal.science/hal-01573916v1/document">Read their paper, Proposition 2 and Theorem 2.</a></p>
  <p>Now let each arrow carry a sign. A signed permutation sends the coordinate vector <var>e</var><sub><var>i</var></sub> to either <var>e</var><sub><var>π</var>(<var>i</var>)</sub> or its negative. A cycle is negative if the product of the signs along its arrows is −1. Can the relabeling <var>Y</var> still be made into a single cycle, and can we prescribe that it be negative? The construction below gives a signed extension of the 1968 theorem. The priority of this precise signed statement is left open.</p>
  <p>The move that makes it possible takes place between two matchings. Write the target as <var>w</var> = <var>XZ</var>, where <var>X</var> and <var>Z</var> square to the identity. Draw their ordinary swaps in red and blue, respectively, and mark any fixed coordinate whose sign they flip. If relabeling by <var>Y</var> carries the red swaps and fixed-coordinate signs to the blue ones, then <var>Z</var> = <var>YXY</var><sup>−1</sup>. Substituting this into <var>XZ</var> gives the commutator. The freedom is in how you relabel the matchings. <a href="/refactors/build-a-commutator#return">See the same matchings before signs are added.</a></p>
<section class="refactor-matching-experiment" aria-labelledby="experiment">
  <h2 id="experiment">Join three cycles into one</h2>
  <p>The diagram fixes the two matchings on seven coordinates. The arrows of <var>Y</var> start in three cycles. Exchange the images of 1 and 2, the ends of a red edge, and of 6 and 7, two positive red fixed coordinates. Try either order. Then flip the sign of <var>Y</var>(7). The signed window of <var>Y</var> changes while all seven entries of the commutator stay fixed.</p>
  <p>Swap the images of 1 and 2 first and you recover the earlier article’s relabeling, with 7 fixed.</p>
